{"name":"Phoenix Tears","hex":"#99b1a2","fetched":"2026-10-16","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"phoenix","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A mythological bird, said to be the only one of its kind, which lives for 500 years and then dies by burning to ashes on a pyre of its own making, ignited by the sun. It then arises anew from the ashes.","Anything that is reborn after apparently being destroyed."]},{"part":"Verb","definitions":["To transfer assets from one company to another to dodge liability"]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/phoenix"},{"term":"tears","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["plural of tear (“liquid from the eyes”)","Viscous streaks left on the inside of the glass when certain wines are swirled around before tasting."]},{"part":"Verb","definitions":["third-person singular simple present indicative of tear"]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/tears"}],"entries":[{"title":"Phoenix (mythology)","blocks":[{"text":"The phoenix is a legendary immortal bird that cyclically regenerates or is otherwise born again. Originating in Greek mythology, it has analogues in many cultures, such as Egyptian and Persian mythology. Associated with the Sun, a phoenix obtains new life by rising from the ashes of its predecessor. Some legends say it dies in a show of flames and combustion, while others say that it simply burns to death and decomposes before being born again. In the Motif-Index of Folk-Literature, a tool used by folklorists, the phoenix is classified as motif B32."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/c4/Phoenix_%281583%29.svg/330px-Phoenix_%281583%29.svg.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Phoenix_(mythology)","wikidata":"Q48444"},{"title":"Tears","blocks":[{"text":"Tears are a transparent fluid secreted primarily by the lacrimal glands found in the eyes of all land mammals. According to the mode of production, tears are classified into four types: basal, closed eye, emotional, and reflex. The basal rate of tear secretion is ~0.5–2.2 μL/min, and irritation can increase secretion by up to ~100-fold, reaching ~300 μL/min. Tears are made up of water, electrolytes, proteins, lipids, and mucins that form layers on the surface of eyes. The four types of tears differ significantly in their composition."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/08/USMC-04952.jpg/330px-USMC-04952.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Tears","wikidata":"Q76469"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/43/Phoenix-Fabelwesen.jpg/500px-Phoenix-Fabelwesen.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/43/Phoenix-Fabelwesen.jpg/500px-Phoenix-Fabelwesen.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"A depiction of a phoenix by Friedrich Justin Bertuch (1806)","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3APhoenix-Fabelwesen.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/5d/2016-09_zoo_sauvage_de_Saint-F%C3%A9licien_-_Grus_virgo_03.jpg/500px-2016-09_zoo_sauvage_de_Saint-F%C3%A9licien_-_Grus_virgo_03.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/5d/2016-09_zoo_sauvage_de_Saint-F%C3%A9licien_-_Grus_virgo_03.jpg/1280px-2016-09_zoo_sauvage_de_Saint-F%C3%A9licien_-_Grus_virgo_03.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/5d/2016-09_zoo_sauvage_de_Saint-F%C3%A9licien_-_Grus_virgo_03.jpg/500px-2016-09_zoo_sauvage_de_Saint-F%C3%A9licien_-_Grus_virgo_03.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"According to the Cata